<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "Declaring a component does not mean it exists. Of the ten declared components one is realised: qr-codec. Component locations are derivation targets, and the import-resolution facet is what distinguishes a declared location from a realised one." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "Fifteen architecture facets are declared blocking and none is yet enforced. An enforcement binding must name the tool, invocation and configuration, and an adversarial verification must show a planted violation failing, before any of them is proof. Until then they are unsatisfied obligations, which is why they are declared APPLICABLE rather than reserved." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "The legacy product spread QR responsibility across domain/qr, domain/render, application/generator and platform/worker with overlapping option surfaces. This states the intended target; it is not a description of the legacy tree, which is behavioural evidence only." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:objective> "ANQR's intended topology stated as its rules. The product is a ports-and-adapters architecture whose centre is artefact meaning and QR mathematics, and whose every delivery surface is a thin inbound adapter over one application layer." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:styleRule> "A delivery surface is a thin translation into the application layer and never a second home for product logic." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:styleRule> "An interface lives beside the layer that needs it, not beside the code that happens to implement it, which makes dependency inversion structural rather than aspirational." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:styleRule> "Everything technology-specific and replaceable sits here, where swapping it cannot reach the centre." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:styleRule> "Naming the style is not declaring it. Every claim this style makes is expressed as a layer, a port, a dependency rule, a boundary or a facet, and anything not so expressed is not part of the architecture." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:styleRule> "One place expresses what a journey does, so web, HTTP, MCP and mobile cannot each invent a different meaning for the same outcome." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
<urn:anqr:architecture:1> <urn:usf:predicate:styleRule> "The centre depends on nothing beyond the language standard library, so it can be reasoned about, tested and reused on every surface without a runtime." <urn:anqr:graph:architecture> .
